-- title:
--   Remark 1 — $C_T\succeq C^M_T$ implies $A_T\succeq A^M_T$, and likewise for $\preceq$
-- statement:
--   Throughout, the standing model of §3.1 holds: $B,\tilde B$ independent Brownian motions with augmented filtrations $\mathcal F\supseteq\mathcal G$, the asset $dX_t=X_t(\mu_t\,dt+\sigma_t\,dB_t)$, the demand component $C$ with $A_T=C_T+\tilde\sigma\tilde B_T$, the two Markov hypotheses, and $\mathbb P^M$ with $d\mathbb P^M/d\mathbb P=Z_T$ (12).
--
--   Suppose $\mathbb P^M$ is a probability measure. Write $X\succeq Y$ for the usual stochastic order, and $C^M_T$, $A^M_T$ for random variables whose laws under $\mathbb P$ are the laws of $C_T$, $A_T$ under $\mathbb P^M$ (p.12, p.17). Then
--
--   1. $C_T\succeq C^M_T$ implies $A_T\succeq A^M_T$;
--   2. $C_T\preceq C^M_T$ implies $A_T\preceq A^M_T$.
--
--   In the tail form used in Lean, (1) reads: if $\mathbb P^M(C_T>x)\le\mathbb P(C_T>x)$ for every real $x$, then $\mathbb P^M(A_T>x)\le\mathbb P(A_T>x)$ for every real $x$; (2) is the same with the two measures exchanged.
--
--   The remark transfers the definition of a positive (negative) impact of the asset trend on demand to the market size, which is what Proposition 4 uses.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The published `StochasticOrders.Usual.usual_order_convolution` (Shaked–Shanthikumar, Theorem 1.A.3(b)) is the closure of the usual order under convolution invoked by the paper.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: $\mathcal F_t$ and $\mathcal G_t$ are the filtrations generated by $(B_t,\tilde B_t)$ and $B_t$ (p.11) → the $\mathbb P$-augmented natural filtrations of $(B,\tilde B)$ and of $B$; reason: martingale representation (Prop. EC.3) holds for the augmented Brownian filtration, and null-set modifications of an adapted process must stay adapted
--   - reading: $(X_t,\mu_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P$ and $(X_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P^M$ (pp.11–12) → the simple Markov property on $[0,T]$ with respect to $\mathcal F$: $\mathbb E_Q[g(Y_t)\mid\mathcal F_s]=\mathbb E_Q[g(Y_t)\mid\sigma(Y_s)]$ a.s. for $s\le t\le T$ and bounded measurable $g$; reason: the paper does not define the term; this is the standard definition, relative to the filtration of footnote 4
--   - reading: $c$ the unit production cost, $s$ the unit salvage value, $X_0$ an asset price → $s<c$ and $x_0>0$ in the parameter structure; reason: the newsvendor convention (p.7), implied by Assumption 3 wherever it is assumed; $X_t>0$ is used on p.ec28
--   - encoding: processes on $[0,T]$ → processes indexed by $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$, constrained only for $t\le T$; reason: Mathlib's Brownian motion lives on $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$; values after $T$ never enter
--   - encoding: stochastic integrals $\int\theta\,dX$, $\int b\,d\tilde B$, $\int\eta\,dB$ with almost-sure integrability → the published relation `EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral` (integrand cut off after $T$, square integrable on every path) plus a pathwise $du$-integral of the drift on every path; reason: under the augmented filtration an integrand can be set to zero on the exceptional null set without losing measurability, so the classes agree up to indistinguishability
--   - reading: for $u\le v$, $C_v-C_u$ is adapted to the σ-algebra generated by $\{X_t,u\le t\le v\}$ (p.11) → $C_v-C_u$ is measurable with respect to $\sigma\{X_t:u\le t\le v\}$, for $u\le v\le T$; reason: "adapted" for a single random variable means measurable
--   - encoding: $Z_t=e^{-\int_0^t\eta_s dB_s-\frac12\int_0^t\eta_s^2ds}$ (12) → $Z_t=\exp(-I_t-\frac12\int_0^t\eta_u^2du)$ with $I$ a $\mathcal G$-adapted version of the Itô integral $\int\eta\,dB$; reason: an Itô integral is fixed only up to a null set; the paper uses that $Z$ is $\mathcal G$-adapted (p.ec23)
--   - encoding: $C_T\succeq C^M_T$: $\mathbb P(C^M_T\le a)=\mathbb P^M(C_T\le a)\ge\mathbb P(C_T\le a)$ for all $a$ (pp.12–13) → `StochasticOrders.Usual.UsualOrder P^M P C_T C_T`: $\mathbb P^M(C_T>x)\le\mathbb P(C_T>x)$ for all $x$; reason: $C^M_T$ enters only through its law; for probability measures the tail form is the distribution-function form
--   - reading: $\mathbb P^M$ the risk-neutral probability measure of (12); Remark 1 lists no assumption → the hypothesis that $\mathbb P^M$ is a probability measure; reason: "risk-neutral probability measure" (p.13) presupposes it; Assumption 5 implies it
-- source:
--   Wang–Yao–Zhang, How Does Risk Hedging Impact Operations? Insights from a Price-Setting Newsvendor Model, arXiv:2201.01026v4, Remark 1, p.13; definition of $\succeq$ and $C^M_T$, p.12

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HedgePSNV_Model
import Definitions.Def_StochasticOrders_Usual_UsualOrder

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace HedgePSNV

/-- Remark 1, arXiv:2201.01026v4, p.13: `C_T ⪰ C^M_T` implies `A_T ⪰ A^M_T`, and
`C_T ⪯ C^M_T` implies `A_T ⪯ A^M_T`, where `X ⪰ Y` is the usual stochastic order (p.12:
`P(X ≤ a) ≤ P(Y ≤ a)` for all real `a`).

`C^M_T` (resp. `A^M_T`) is a random variable whose law under `P` is the law of `C_T`
(resp. `A_T`) under `P^M` (p.12), so the order is stated between `C_T` under `P^M` and
`C_T` under `P`, via `StochasticOrders.Usual.UsualOrder` (`μ{x < X} ≤ ν{x < Y}` for all `x`).

**Formalization Note** The remark presupposes that the risk-neutral measure `P^M` of (12) is a
probability measure; this is the hypothesis `IsProbabilityMeasure M.PM`. For probability
measures the tail form of `UsualOrder` is the paper's distribution-function form. -/
theorem remark_1 (p : Params) {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (M : Model p Ω)
    (hM : M.Standing) (hPM : IsProbabilityMeasure M.PM) :
    (StochasticOrders.Usual.UsualOrder M.PM M.P (M.C p.T) (M.C p.T) →
      StochasticOrders.Usual.UsualOrder M.PM M.P M.AT M.AT) ∧
    (StochasticOrders.Usual.UsualOrder M.P M.PM (M.C p.T) (M.C p.T) →
      StochasticOrders.Usual.UsualOrder M.P M.PM M.AT M.AT) := by sorry

end HedgePSNV
